Super user rights are usually restricted to the root account. This means you
will have to log in to a root shell to make any changes to your system or run
commands through su, which does not cache credentials. For this reason, most
systems make use of a helper application to run commands with elevated
privileges as a regular user.

BSD's doas has been gaining traction on Linux as it is simple and easy to set
up. Its feature set is by design very small and does not offer much in terms of
customization. The more established sudo falls on the other side of the scale,
offering more features than most home users will probably ever need.

This script uses sudo-rs, a drop-in and memory safe replacement for sudo written
in Rust. sudo-rs is a more modern alternative to sudo that offers greater
customization options than doas. We will use a common usage pattern when setting
up sudo. All users belonging to the wheel group will be able to run any command
with elevated privileges but will be asked to enter their password upon running
sudo.
